  "status": "open_not_proven",
  "theorem_name": "DyadicGapTwoEquivalenceAndFiniteGapAggregateNoGo",
  "declared_proposition": "Let T_{j,h} count prime pairs p,p+h with 2^j<=p<2^{j+1}, for even h in a fixed finite set H containing 2. The twin-prime conjecture is equivalent to T_{j,2}>0 for infinitely many j. If sum_{h in H}T_{j,h}>0 for infinitely many j, finite pigeonhole only implies that some h in H is positive infinitely often; it does not select h=2. The exact countermodel T_{j,6}=1 and T_{j,h}=0 for h!=6 has positive bounded-gap aggregate on every block and no gap-two mass.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Every twin pair belongs to one dyadic block, and each bounded block contains finitely many integers, so infinitely many twin pairs are equivalent to gap-two positivity on infinitely many dyadic indices. For a finite channel set H, infinitely many positive aggregate blocks contain infinitely many occurrences distributed among finitely many h; one channel therefore recurs infinitely often. Nothing in this pigeonhole statement identifies that channel as two, as the gap-six countermodel proves. Thus bounded-gap positivity and even exact aggregate mass require a gap-two channel-isolation input.",
